A violation of Texas Deceptive Trade PracticesConsumer Protection Act ( DTPA ) Tex. Bus. & Com. Code 17.46 ( b ) ( 5 ), ( 7 ), ( 24 ) Prohibits misrepresenting the terms of an agreement, failing to disclose information, and taking advantage of a consumers lack of knowledge.

A violation of 15 U.S. Code 1692b - Acquisition of location information - ( 2 ) not state that such consumer owes any debt ; ( 5 ) not use any language or symbol on any envelope or in the contents of any communication effected by the mails or telegram that indicates that the debt collector is in the debt collection business or that the communication relates to the collection of a debt ; Conclusion : The NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY engaged in deceptive and unfair trade practices by securitizing Plaintiffs retail installment sales contract and embedding my personal and financial identifiers into Asset-Backed Securities sold to third-party investors without my knowledge or consent. This practice misrepresented the nature of the credit relationship, exceeded the scope of permissible purposes under 15 U.S.C. 1681b, violated the privacy protections of 15 U.S.C. 68016809, and constituted an unauthorized transfer of consumers means of identification in violation of 18 U.S.C. 1028. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY 's actions resulted in unjust enrichment by monetizing my identity and contractual obligations as a tradable investment product.

C. - INSURANCE FRAUD, MISREPRESENTATION & DECEPTION - Facts to support complaint When you buy a car through dealer-arranged financing, the dealership sells your Retail Installment Sales Contract ( RISC ) to a finance company or bank at or near the time of sale.

The finance company pays the dealership the full amount financed upfront ( minus any dealer reserve or buy rate adjustment ).

This means the dealer has been paid in full immediately for the vehicle theres no waiting on the consumers payments.

NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY and many others alike induce a consumer into default early in the loan, in that event the lender will : Repossess your vehicle.

Apply any auction sale proceeds or insurance payout to the account.

Claim the remaining loan balance as a loss on paper.

It is key to note that even greater deception takes place when that loss is not truly a loss at all, especially when the lender has not only paid themself in full through In-House-Financing but also has already recovered the full principal value through the original sale of the contract plus other sources.

1. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY In-House-Financed the vehicle purchase for the myself, XXXX XXXX XXXX. 

2. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY sold me GAP insurance, under the misrepresentation that it is mandatory and through deceptive sales practices. The premium was then rolled into my loan amount.

3. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY deliberately accelerated default on the loan by : Misrepresenting the applying of payments to late fees or other charges instead of interest.

Failing to provide proper credit for overpayments.

Implementing unfair or undisclosed loan servicing practices.

4. NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY then initiated insurance fraud by recording a Write Off and Total Loss of the total balance of the vehicle. This is not only a tax benefit to NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY but also activated the GAP Insurance, which in turn pays the "" gap '' amount ( the difference between the vehicle 's value and the remaining loan balance ) to the creditor, not the consumer. This would constitute as a Double Dip : The deception and fraud lies in NISSAN MOTOR ACCEPTANCE COMPANY recovering both through the tax write-off AND the GAP insurance payout for the same debt, effectively "" double-dipping ''.

Consumer 's Continued Burden : Despite the loan company 's recovery, consumer are still held responsible for remaining balances, collection fees, or negative marks on their credit report.

