15253899,2025-08-14,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Information belongs to someone else,"On XX/XX/year>, I provided TransUnion, Equifax, and Experian with a police report and a proof of data breach letter documenting identity theft. My correspondence included a written dispute of specific accounts appearing on my credit file, with the express request for removal in accordance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ), 15 U.S.C. 1681 et seq.

Pursuant to FCRA 611 ( a ) ( 1 ) ( A ), consumer reporting agencies are required to conduct a reasonable investigation of disputed information and provide the results of such investigation to the consumer within 30 days ( extendable to 45 days if additional information is provided ). Furthermore, FCRA 611 ( a ) ( 6 ) requires that, upon completion of the investigation, the agencies provide written notice of the determination, including the method of verification. 



It has now been over 60 days since my submission, yet : The disputed items remain on my credit reports. 
No proof of accuracy, verification, or detailed investigation results have been provided. 
This constitutes a failure to comply with both FCRA 611 ( failure to complete investigation and provide results ) and FCRA 623 ( failure of furnishers to accurately report information ). 




